---
// Custom CHS case study. Replaces the Notion render. Notion still drives
// metadata (title, tags, cover, order) for the homepage list; only the
// body content is custom.
//
// Narrative spine: documentation IS product. The docs have to live where
// the work happens — and teach the tool by being the tool. Sections are
// moments of a non-technical employee at a 10,000-person co-op meeting an
// internal AI platform for the first time.

import CSLayout from "../../components/case-study/CSLayout.astro";
import CSHero from "../../components/case-study/CSHero.astro";
import CSSection from "../../components/case-study/CSSection.astro";
import CSPullquote from "../../components/case-study/CSPullquote.astro";
import CSStatGrid from "../../components/case-study/CSStatGrid.astro";
import CSLiveDemo from "../../components/case-study/CSLiveDemo.astro";
import CSFigure from "../../components/case-study/CSFigure.astro";
import ChsRolePicker from "../../components/case-study/showcase/ChsRolePicker.astro";

export const prerender = true;
---

<CSLayout
  title="CHS — Jon Tollefson"
  description="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees. The internal AI platform shipped — then the comprehension surface had to be designed. Sole UX function, embedded with engineering."
  ogImage="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
>
  <CSHero
    eyebrow="Case study 01 · November 2025 — present"
    title="CHS"
    subtitle="Teaching AI to 10,000 employees."
    cover="/case-studies/chs/cover.png"
    meta={[
      { label: "Role", value: "Sole UX function, embedded with engineering" },
      { label: "Timeline", value: "Nov 2025 — present (launched Mar 2026)" },
      { label: "Stack", value: "Open WebUI · Azure · Figma" },
      { label: "Status", value: "Shipped · company-wide rollout in progress" },
    ]}
  />

  {/* ─────── 01 · THE PITCH ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="01" title="The pitch" deck="CHS is a 10,000-person agricultural cooperative. ChatCHS is the internal AI platform: knowledge bases, custom models, function-calling, all of it grounded in CHS data. The platform shipped. The audience didn't." noTopRule>
    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The catch was never the platform. Sales, GTM, finance, ops — smart people across the company who should not need to know what an embedding is to get value from AI at work. The gap between what the tool could do and what a non-technical user could reach was the entire UX problem. That gap was where senior design earned its keep.</p>

      <p>I came in as the sole UX function on the platform team, embedded with engineering. Pre-launch, we ran a beta with the 250 employees already licensed for GPT Enterprise: power users in name, diagnostic cohort in practice. The team's first reflex was "we need more docs." My read was different. The docs themselves had to do the work of teaching, not document what already existed.</p>

      <CSPullquote hero>
        Documentation is product. When a team's first instinct for "users don't get it" is to write a Confluence page, you've already lost.
      </CSPullquote>
    </div>

    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 10000, label: "employees in the target audience", color: "white" },
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort, pre-launch", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 3, label: "friction patterns surfaced", color: "yellow" },
        { n: 1, label: "comprehension surface to design", color: "pink" },
      ]}
    />
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 02 · THE COMPREHENSION SURFACE ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="02" title="The comprehension surface" deck="On the left, what ChatCHS can do. On the right, what a non-technical user can actually grasp. The space between is the entire design problem.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/chs/notion-35fed8d4704f80ff87d5e0f10aa01f6d.png"
      alt="The comprehension surface diagram. Tool capability on the left (embeddings, RAG, function calling, custom models), audience reach on the right (what does this do for me, is this different from GPT, where do I start, can it just do the thing). Between them, a labeled surface: task-flow docs, starter templates, differentiation messaging, dogfooded docs-chat."
      caption="Diagnosed from biweekly research with power users. Three patterns surfaced: role disconnect (users couldn't tell what ChatCHS could do for their specific role), tool confusion (no way to choose between ChatCHS and the GPT Enterprise license they already had), and setup friction (custom KBs needed a back-channel ping to engineering — self-serve in name only)."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The comprehension surface is the deliverable. It is not a Confluence page, a video tutorial, or a wiki structure. It is a set of interaction patterns that live inside the tool itself, designed so the docs and the platform are the same gesture. The work after diagnosis was to build that surface piece by piece.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 03 · INVENTORY VS TASK FLOW ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="03" title="Feature inventory vs task flow" deck="A feature inventory tells you what exists. A task flow tells you how to get something done. Engineers need the first one. Non-technical users need the second. The platform shipped with only the first.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/chs/notion-35fed8d4704f808d948ffbd7b51ca834.png"
      alt="Two columns side by side. Left, 'Feature inventory' for engineers: knowledge bases, models, functions, prompts, memory, tools, integrations. Right, 'Task flow' for non-technical users: set up your team's knowledge base, draft a proposal in CHS voice, query CHS data conversationally, summarize meeting notes, build a custom model. An arrow labeled 'reframe' connects the two."
      caption="The Getting Started docs got rebuilt from the ground up around tasks, not features. Same platform, different organizing logic. The reframe arrow is the entire pedagogical move."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Research with power users happened biweekly through the pre-launch beta. The findings drove the structure: where people got stuck, the syntax experienced users had figured out the hard way, the prompts that actually worked. All of that fed directly into the task-flow docs and the starter templates we shipped.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 04 · ROLE-AWARE START ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="04" title="Role-aware starter surface" deck="The single sharpest friction in the beta was 'I don't know what this is for, for me.' The fix: each role lands in a docs-chat whose starter prompts are already in their own language. Pick a role on the left — the chat below reframes itself.">
    <CSLiveDemo
      label="LIVE · PICK A ROLE"
      caption="Sales gets starter prompts about deal-review KBs and follow-up drafts. Finance gets variance summaries and board narratives. Ops gets SOP conversion and field-Spanish translation. The platform is the same. The way it introduces itself isn't. Click a starter — the chat shows the kind of response the docs-chat actually returns."
      bleed
      pad="comfy"
    >
      <ChsRolePicker />
    </CSLiveDemo>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Differentiation messaging belongs in this same surface. The other live frame in ChatCHS during the beta was "is this different from the GPT Enterprise license I already have?" Each role's starter set includes one starter that names the comparison directly — <code>Compare with GPT Ent.</code> as a quick prompt — so the question gets answered the moment it surfaces, in plain language, inside the tool.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 05 · DOGFOOD THE DOCS ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="05" title="Dogfood the docs" deck="The docs-chat uses ChatCHS to teach ChatCHS. Template prompts live in the chat. Embedded resources sit one click away. Users learn the mental model by inhabiting it, not by studying it.">
    <CSFigure
      src="/case-studies/chs/notion-35fed8d4704f8047b0fac992a9e05d5f.png"
      alt="The ChatCHS docs assistant interface. A user message 'Set up a KB for my GTM team?' sits in the top right. Below, the docs assistant replies 'Let's walk through it. First, point ChatCHS at the files your team already uses.' followed by a highlighted embedded-resource pill: 'Template · embedded resource — Build a KB from our shared GTM Drive folder, tagged by quarter, with weekly auto-refresh.' Quick prompts at the bottom: 'Start a KB', 'Use a template', 'Compare with GPT Ent.' Two callouts: 'Template prompts live in the chat' and 'The chat uses the platform's own features to teach them.'"
      caption="Teaching a tool by using the tool short-circuits the gap between 'read the manual' and 'try it.' The chat itself is a working ChatCHS conversation. Every gesture the user learns here transfers verbatim to their own KB build the next morning."
      width="wide"
      framed
    />

    <CSPullquote cite="Reflection, post-launch">
      The dogfooded docs-chat is the move I'd most repeat. Users learn the mental model by inhabiting it, not studying it.
    </CSPullquote>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 06 · WHAT SHIPPED ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="06" title="What shipped" deck="Since the March launch, access has broadened past the original beta. For the first time, departmental users are setting up custom models grounded in internal CHS databases themselves.">
    <CSStatGrid
      stats={[
        { n: 250, label: "beta cohort", suffix: " →", color: "white" },
        { n: 10000, label: "company-wide reach (rolling)", color: "green" },
        { n: 0, label: "back-channel pings to engineering needed", color: "cyan" },
        { n: 4, label: "interaction patterns shipped as the comprehension surface", color: "yellow" },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>The four interaction patterns that make up the comprehension surface — task-flow docs, role-aware starters, differentiation messaging, the dogfooded docs-chat — ship as part of the platform now. They are the platform's onboarding gesture. They are also the design system other internal teams have started to inherit when they build their own surfaces on top of Open WebUI.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  {/* ─────── 07 · WHAT GENERALIZES ─────── */}
  <CSSection num="07" title="What generalizes" deck="Three things I'd carry into any enterprise AI tool with a non-technical audience.">
    <ul class="generalize">
      <li>
        <strong>Documentation is product.</strong>
        The docs have to live where the work happens. The interaction patterns of the docs and the interaction patterns of the tool should be the same patterns. If the docs and the tool look different, the docs are losing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Teach by inhabiting.</strong>
        A dogfooded docs-chat short-circuits the gap between "read the manual" and "try it." Every gesture the user learns in the docs transfers verbatim to the next morning's actual KB build. That is the cheapest pedagogy you can buy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The bottleneck is rarely the AI.</strong>
        On enterprise AI tools, the AI is fine. The bottleneck is the comprehension surface between capability and audience. Role disconnect, tool confusion, setup friction — those are UX problems, and that gap is where senior design earns its keep.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="prose-block">
      <p>Meta-note: the site you're reading is the same approach in miniature. Notion as content store, database rows as case-study variants, one-attribute theme cycling, primitives reused across pages. The Tolo.Cloud case study takes it further. The Colophon documents how it's built.</p>
      <p class="muted">Specifics are kept high-level out of respect for ongoing project confidentiality. Happy to walk through the rest in conversation.</p>
    </div>
  </CSSection>

  <div class="cs-foot">
    <a href="/#work" class="cs-foot-back">← back to selected work</a>
  </div>
</CSLayout>

<style>
  .prose-block { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block > .cs-pullquote { max-width: 64ch; }
  .prose-block .muted { color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.48); font-size: 14.5px; }

  .generalize {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 18px 0 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 64ch;
  }
  .generalize li {
    padding: 16px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--cs-line);
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.02);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: rgba(245, 241, 232, 0.85);
  }
  .generalize li strong {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .cs-foot {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 64px 0 0;
  }
  .cs-foot-back {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--brand-orange);
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.32);
    border-radius: 2px;
    transition: color 160ms ease, border-color 160ms ease, background 160ms ease;
  }
  .cs-foot-back:hover {
    color: #f5f1e8;
    border-color: var(--brand-orange);
    background: rgba(255, 90, 60, 0.08);
  }
</style>
